window.SCENARIOS = [{"topic":"Memos","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"The task restarted and every note disappeared","q":"<p>The ECS task for Memos was replaced overnight (a deploy, or Fargate moved it). When users log back in, <strong>all their notes are gone</strong>. What happened, and how would you fix it?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Memos uses <strong>SQLite by default</strong>, and the Dockerfile sets <code>MEMOS_DATA=/var/opt/memos</code>. That folder lives on the task's <strong>ephemeral storage</strong>. The task definition mounts no volume.</li>\n<li>When a task stops, its storage is deleted. A new task starts with an empty database. <strong>Every deploy wipes the data.</strong></li>\n<li>Fix, in order of preference:<ol>\n<li><strong>Use a managed database.</strong> Memos supports MySQL and Postgres through <code>MEMOS_DRIVER</code> and <code>MEMOS_DSN</code>. Run <strong>RDS Postgres (Multi-AZ)</strong> in private subnets, allow 5432 only from the ECS security group, and pass the DSN from <strong>Secrets Manager</strong> through the task definition's <code>secrets</code>.</li>\n<li>Or mount <strong>EFS</strong> at <code>/var/opt/memos</code> (an <code>efs_volume_configuration</code> in the task definition and a mount target in each private subnet). This is quick, but SQLite on a network file system must only ever have <strong>one writer</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Add <strong>backups</strong>: RDS automated backups and snapshots, or AWS Backup for EFS.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>This is the biggest known limit in the project, and interviewers love it because it tests whether you understand <strong>stateless vs stateful</strong> containers. Say it before they find it: “containers are disposable, so state has to live outside them.”</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile","label":"Dockerfile"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"}],"id":"s-the-task-restarted-and-every-note-disapp-0"},{"topic":"AWS","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Make Memos highly available","q":"<p>The network already spans <strong>two AZs</strong>. Is Memos actually highly available? What would you change?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>No, not yet.</strong> <code>desired_count = 1</code>, so there is one task in one AZ. If that task or AZ fails, the site is down until ECS starts a replacement (usually a minute or two, and the SQLite data is lost too).</li>\n<li>The network is ready for HA: two public and two private subnets, a NAT gateway in <strong>each</strong> AZ, and an ALB across both.</li>\n<li>Changes:<ol>\n<li>Move the data out of the container (RDS Multi-AZ). Several tasks can't share one local SQLite file.</li>\n<li>Set <code>desired_count = 2</code> or more. ECS spreads tasks across the private subnets, so each AZ gets one.</li>\n<li>Add <strong>ECS service auto scaling</strong> (target tracking on CPU around 60–70%, or on ALB request count per target).</li>\n<li>Add the <strong>deployment circuit breaker with rollback</strong>, and set <code>minimum_healthy_percent = 100</code> so a deploy never drops below full capacity.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>It shows you know the difference between a multi-AZ <strong>network</strong> and a multi-AZ <strong>application</strong>. The network was already in place; the data layer and task count were not.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/vpc/main.tf"}],"id":"s-make-memos-highly-available-1"},{"topic":"Troubleshooting","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"The pipeline says healthy, but the target group says unhealthy","q":"<p><code>deploy.yml</code> checks <code>https://tm.myegh.com/healthz</code> and passes, but last week the ALB marked the task unhealthy while the app was clearly running. Why can these disagree?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>They check <strong>different things</strong>. The pipeline curls <strong><code>/healthz</code></strong> once, 60 seconds after the deploy. The target group's health check requests <strong><code>/</code></strong> on port 8081 every <strong>30 s</strong>, accepts <strong>200–399</strong>, and needs <strong>2</strong> failures to go unhealthy.</li>\n<li><code>/</code> is the web UI. It can redirect or be slow while assets load, so it measures more than “is the app alive”. Memos has a real <code>GET /healthz</code> endpoint in <code>server/server.go</code>.</li>\n<li>Fix: point the target group's <code>health_check.path</code> at <strong><code>/healthz</code></strong> so the ALB and the pipeline agree. Also give a slow-starting task a <strong><code>health_check_grace_period_seconds</code></strong> on the ECS service.</li>\n<li>Debug order: target group health reason → ECS service events → task stopped reason → logs in <code>/ecs/memos</code>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>It tests whether you know <strong>who decides health</strong>. The ALB does: it only sends traffic to healthy targets, and ECS replaces tasks the ALB reports as unhealthy.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/alb/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/alb/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml","label":".github/workflows/deploy.yml"}],"id":"s-the-pipeline-says-healthy-but-the-target-2"},{"topic":"Troubleshooting","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","title":"503 from the load balancer right after a deploy","q":"<p>Straight after a deploy, <code>tm.myegh.com</code> returns <strong>503 Service Temporarily Unavailable</strong>. Walk me through how you'd find the cause.</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>A 503 from an ALB means <strong>no healthy targets are registered</strong>. The ALB itself is fine. The task is either not running or failing health checks.</li>\n<li>Check, layer by layer:<ol>\n<li><strong>ECS service events</strong>: is the task starting and then stopping? Read the <strong>stopped reason</strong> (e.g. <code>CannotPullContainerError</code>, or exit code 137 for out of memory).</li>\n<li><strong>CloudWatch logs</strong> <code>/ecs/memos</code>: did the app crash on start?</li>\n<li><strong>Port</strong>: is it 8081 everywhere? That means <code>MEMOS_PORT</code> in the Dockerfile, <code>containerPort</code> in the task definition, the target group port and both security group rules. (I hit exactly this: 5230 vs 8081.)</li>\n<li><strong>Security groups</strong>: the ECS SG must allow 8081 <strong>from the ALB SG</strong>, and the ALB SG must allow <strong>egress</strong> to the ECS SG on 8081.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>If the last version worked, <strong>roll back first</strong> (redeploy the previous SHA), then find the root cause.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Knowing <strong>502 vs 503 vs 504</strong> quickly narrows the search: 502 is a bad reply from the target, 503 is no healthy targets, and 504 is the target taking too long to reply.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/security/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile","label":"Dockerfile"}],"id":"s-503-from-the-load-balancer-right-after-a-3"},{"topic":"CI/CD","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Roll back a bad release in under five minutes","q":"<p>A release has gone out and users are seeing errors. How do you roll back with this pipeline, and how would you make it automatic?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Every image is tagged with the <strong>full commit SHA</strong>, and ECR tags are <strong>IMMUTABLE</strong>, so an old tag always means exactly the same image.</li>\n<li><code>deploy.yml</code> has a <strong><code>workflow_dispatch</code></strong> input, <code>image_tag</code>. Run it with the <strong>previous good SHA</strong>. It checks that the tag is 40 hex characters and exists in ECR, then runs <code>terraform plan/apply</code> with that <code>image_url</code>. ECS rolls the service back to the old image.</li>\n<li>To make it automatic, add <code>deployment_circuit_breaker { enable = true, rollback = true }</code> to the ECS service. If new tasks can't become healthy, ECS returns to the last working deployment by itself.</li>\n<li>Then fix forward in a normal PR.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>“Roll back first, debug second”</strong> is what interviewers want to hear. Immutable SHA tags are what make a rollback safe.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml","label":".github/workflows/deploy.yml"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/build.yml","label":".github/workflows/build.yml"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecr/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecr/main.tf"}],"id":"s-roll-back-a-bad-release-in-under-five-mi-4"},{"topic":"Security","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Your OIDC role has AdministratorAccess","q":"<p>A security reviewer points out that <code>github-actions-role</code> has <strong>AdministratorAccess</strong>, and its trust policy allows <code>repo:EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra:*</code>. What's the risk, and what would you change?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Risk 1: too much permission.</strong> Anything that runs in a workflow can do <em>anything</em> in the account, including deleting the state bucket or creating users.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk 2: a wide trust policy.</strong> <code>:*</code> matches <strong>any branch, tag, pull request or environment</strong> in the repo. A workflow on any branch could assume an admin role.</li>\n<li>Fix:<ol>\n<li>Narrow <code>sub</code> to <code>repo:EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra:ref:refs/heads/main</code>, or better, <code>...:environment:production</code> with a protected GitHub environment.</li>\n<li>Replace AdministratorAccess with a <strong>least-privilege policy</strong>: ECR push to the <code>memos</code> repo, read/write on the state bucket key, and the specific ECS/ELB/EC2/IAM/ACM/Route 53 actions Terraform needs, scoped by tag or ARN where possible.</li>\n<li>Split roles: a <strong>read-only plan role</strong> for pull requests, and an <strong>apply role</strong> only for <code>main</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Keep what's already good: OIDC means <strong>no long-lived keys</strong>, and the <code>aud = sts.amazonaws.com</code> check is correct.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>OIDC is one of the strongest parts of the project. Showing you also know its <strong>weak point</strong> proves you understand it rather than having copied it.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/oidc.tf","label":"bootstrap/oidc.tf"}],"id":"s-your-oidc-role-has-administratoraccess-5"},{"topic":"AWS","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"The AWS bill is higher than expected","q":"<p>The Memos bill is much higher than you expected for one small Fargate task. Where is the money going, and what would you cut?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>The biggest fixed cost is <strong>two NAT gateways</strong> (one per AZ). Each is charged <strong>per hour plus per GB</strong> processed, whether or not traffic flows. That's roughly $35 a month each in eu-west-1 before data.</li>\n<li>Next: the <strong>ALB</strong> (hourly plus LCUs), the <strong>Elastic IPs</strong> (public IPv4 is charged), and the Fargate task (0.25 vCPU, 512 MB, which is small).</li>\n<li>Options:<ul>\n<li>Non-production: <strong>one NAT gateway</strong>, or none (<code>destroy.yml</code> when you aren't using it).</li>\n<li><strong>VPC endpoints</strong>: an S3 gateway endpoint is free and carries ECR image layers. Interface endpoints for ECR API/DKR and CloudWatch Logs cost money per hour, so compare them with the NAT cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Fargate Spot</strong> for non-critical environments.</li>\n<li>Set <strong>AWS Budgets</strong> alerts so you hear about it early.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The trade-off: one NAT gateway saves money but makes it a <strong>single point of failure</strong> for outbound traffic from both AZs.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Cost awareness is part of the Well-Architected Framework, and the NAT gateway is the classic hidden cost. Knowing it from your own bill sounds real.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/vpc/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/alb/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/alb/main.tf"}],"id":"s-the-aws-bill-is-higher-than-expected-6"},{"topic":"Terraform","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Two people run terraform apply at the same time","q":"<p>You and a colleague both run <code>terraform apply</code> on Memos at the same moment. What happens?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>The backend is S3 with <strong><code>use_lockfile = true</code></strong> (S3 native locking, with no DynamoDB table). The first apply writes a <code>.tflock</code> object next to <code>prod/terraform.tfstate</code>. The second fails with <strong>“Error acquiring the state lock”</strong> and changes nothing.</li>\n<li>When the first apply finishes, the lock is removed and the second person can <strong>plan again</strong> against the new state.</li>\n<li>Only use <code>terraform force-unlock LOCK_ID</code> if you're <strong>sure</strong> no apply is running (e.g. a CI job was killed mid-run).</li>\n<li>The state bucket also has <strong>versioning</strong> (to recover an old state), <strong>AES256 encryption</strong>, <strong>public access blocked</strong> and a policy that <strong>denies non-HTTPS</strong> access.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Locking stops two applies from corrupting the state. Mentioning that native S3 locking replaced DynamoDB shows your knowledge is current.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/provider.tf","label":"infra/provider.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/state.tf","label":"bootstrap/state.tf"}],"id":"s-two-people-run-terraform-apply-at-the-sa-7"},{"topic":"Terraform","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Someone changed a security group in the Console","q":"<p>During an incident, someone opened port 22 on the ECS security group in the Console. What happens on the next deploy, and how do you stop this happening?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>That's <strong>drift</strong>: the real infrastructure no longer matches the code. The next <code>terraform plan</code> in <code>deploy.yml</code> shows the extra rule being <strong>removed</strong>, and <code>apply</code> removes it.</li>\n<li>My security group rules are separate <code>aws_vpc_security_group_ingress_rule</code>/<code>egress_rule</code> resources. A rule made in the Console isn't in state at all, so Terraform may not even notice it. Use <code>terraform plan -refresh-only</code> to see the drift, then either <strong>import</strong> it into code (if it should stay) or delete it.</li>\n<li>Prevention: no write access in the Console for normal users, changes <strong>only through pull requests</strong>, AWS Config or CloudTrail alerts on security group changes, and a <strong>scheduled plan</strong> in CI that fails if there's any drift.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>It shows you treat <strong>Terraform as the source of truth</strong>, and that you know <em>how</em> it detects drift and where it can't see it.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/security/main.tf"}],"id":"s-someone-changed-a-security-group-in-the--8"},{"topic":"CI/CD","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Infra changes aren't being deployed","q":"<p>You change <code>infra/modules/alb/main.tf</code>, merge to <code>main</code>, and nothing deploys. Why?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><code>build.yml</code> only runs on pushes to <code>main</code> that touch <strong><code>app/**</code>, <code>Dockerfile</code> or <code>.dockerignore</code></strong>. <code>deploy.yml</code> runs on <strong><code>workflow_run</code></strong> after “Build and Push” succeeds, or by hand.</li>\n<li>A change only under <code>infra/**</code> doesn't trigger the build, so the deploy never runs.</li>\n<li>Today: run <strong>Deploy</strong> by hand (<code>workflow_dispatch</code>) with the SHA of the image that's currently live.</li>\n<li>Better:<ol>\n<li>A <strong><code>terraform plan</code> on every pull request</strong> that touches <code>infra/**</code>, with the plan posted as a comment for review.</li>\n<li>Deploy on <code>push</code> to <code>main</code> for <code>infra/**</code> as well, using the current image tag. That tag could come from an SSM parameter or the current task definition.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>It proves you actually know how your pipeline's triggers work, and it leads naturally to <strong>plan-on-PR</strong>, a common interview follow-up.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/build.yml","label":".github/workflows/build.yml"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml","label":".github/workflows/deploy.yml"}],"id":"s-infra-changes-aren-t-being-deployed-9"},{"topic":"Docker","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","title":"The image scan found a critical vulnerability","q":"<p>ECR <strong>scan on push</strong> reports a critical CVE in the latest Memos image. What do you do?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Read the finding: is it in the <strong>base image</strong> (Alpine 3.21 packages) or in the <strong>app</strong> (a Go module)?</li>\n<li>Base image: rebuild on a patched <code>alpine:3.21</code> tag (or newer), or <code>apk upgrade</code> the affected package. Go module: bump it in <code>go.mod</code> and rebuild.</li>\n<li>Push. The new commit gets a <strong>new SHA tag</strong>, because tags are immutable. Deploy through the normal pipeline.</li>\n<li>What already limits the damage:<ul>\n<li>The <strong>three-stage build</strong>: the runtime image holds only the binary, CA certificates and tzdata, with no compilers, shell tools or source. That's 75.8 MB instead of 2.79 GB.</li>\n<li>It runs as <strong>non-root user 1001</strong>.</li>\n<li>The task runs in <strong>private subnets</strong>, reachable only through the ALB on 8081.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Improve: scan in CI <strong>before</strong> pushing (e.g. Trivy) and fail the build on criticals.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>It links <strong>image size to security</strong>: fewer packages means fewer CVEs, and it shows you have a patching process, not just a scanner.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/Dockerfile","label":"Dockerfile"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecr/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecr/main.tf"}],"id":"s-the-image-scan-found-a-critical-vulnerab-10"},{"topic":"Security","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Add a secret to Memos","q":"<p>You move Memos to RDS and now need to give it a database password. Where does the password go, and which role needs permission?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Store it in <strong>Secrets Manager</strong> (rotation) or <strong>SSM Parameter Store SecureString</strong>, never in <code>terraform.tfvars</code>, the Dockerfile or plain <code>environment</code> variables.</li>\n<li>In the task definition, use <strong><code>secrets = [{ name = \"MEMOS_DSN\", valueFrom = &lt;secret ARN&gt; }]</code></strong>. ECS fetches the secret when the task starts and injects it as an environment variable.</li>\n<li>The <strong>execution role</strong> needs <code>secretsmanager:GetSecretValue</code> on that one secret (plus <code>kms:Decrypt</code> if it uses a customer key). The <strong>task role</strong> doesn't, because ECS fetches it, not the app.</li>\n<li>Today in Memos: the execution role has <code>AmazonECSTaskExecutionRolePolicy</code> (pull from ECR, write logs), and the task role has <strong>no permissions</strong> because the app doesn't call AWS.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Execution role vs task role</strong> is one of the most common ECS interview questions. Answering it with your own roles makes it concrete.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/iam/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/iam/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"}],"id":"s-add-a-secret-to-memos-11"},{"topic":"Troubleshooting","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","title":"The task is stuck: CannotPullContainerError","q":"<p>The service keeps launching tasks that stop with <strong>CannotPullContainerError</strong>. What could cause it in this design?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Tasks run in <strong>private subnets with no public IP</strong>, so they reach ECR through the <strong>NAT gateway</strong>. Check:<ol>\n<li><strong>Route</strong>: the private route table sends <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> to the NAT gateway in the same AZ.</li>\n<li><strong>Security group</strong>: the ECS SG allows <strong>outbound 443</strong> (it does: <code>ecs_https</code>). Without it, nothing can be pulled.</li>\n<li><strong>Execution role</strong>: it has <code>AmazonECSTaskExecutionRolePolicy</code> (<code>ecr:GetAuthorizationToken</code>, <code>BatchGetImage</code>, …).</li>\n<li><strong>Image</strong>: the exact SHA tag exists in the <code>memos</code> repo. <code>deploy.yml</code> checks this with <code>describe-images</code> before applying.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Alternative without NAT: <strong>VPC endpoints</strong> for <code>ecr.api</code>, <code>ecr.dkr</code>, <code>logs</code>, plus an <strong>S3 gateway endpoint</strong> (image layers are stored in S3).</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>It tests whether you understand <strong>how a private container reaches AWS services</strong>: routing, security groups, IAM and the registry all have to line up.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/vpc/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/security/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/iam/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/iam/main.tf"}],"id":"s-the-task-is-stuck-cannotpullcontainererr-12"},{"topic":"CI/CD","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Re-running the pipeline fails: tag already exists","q":"<p>You re-run a failed pipeline on the same commit and the push fails because <strong>the tag already exists</strong>. Why, and how did you fix it?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>The ECR repository has <code>image_tag_mutability = \"IMMUTABLE\"</code>. Once <code>memos:&lt;sha&gt;</code> exists, it can never be overwritten, so a second <code>docker push</code> of the same tag is rejected.</li>\n<li>Fix in <code>build.yml</code>: call <strong><code>aws ecr describe-images</code></strong> for that SHA first.<ul>\n<li>If it exists, <strong>reuse</strong> it and skip the build.</li>\n<li>If the error is <code>ImageNotFoundException</code>, build and push.</li>\n<li>For any other error (e.g. permissions), <strong>fail loudly</strong> instead of guessing.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The workflow outputs the image URI either way, so deploys stay consistent.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Immutable tags are a deliberate safety feature. The fix shows you made the pipeline <strong>idempotent</strong> (safe to re-run) instead of turning the safety off.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/build.yml","label":".github/workflows/build.yml"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecr/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecr/main.tf"}],"id":"s-re-running-the-pipeline-fails-tag-alread-13"},{"topic":"Terraform","level":"Senior","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Your Terraform state bucket was deleted","q":"<p>Someone tries to delete the <code>memos-terraform-state-enamul</code> bucket. What protects you, and how would you recover?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong><code>prevent_destroy = true</code></strong> on the bucket means Terraform refuses to destroy it, even with <code>terraform destroy</code> in the bootstrap stack.</li>\n<li>It lives in a separate <strong>bootstrap</strong> stack with <strong>local state</strong>, so <code>destroy.yml</code> (which destroys <code>infra/</code>) can't touch it.</li>\n<li><strong>Versioning</strong> is on. If the state <em>file</em> is overwritten or deleted, restore the previous version of <code>prod/terraform.tfstate</code>.</li>\n<li>If the whole bucket really is gone (deleted outside Terraform): recreate it with bootstrap, then rebuild state with <strong><code>import</code> blocks</strong> for the existing resources, or <code>terraform destroy</code> by hand in the Console and re-apply. Prevent it next time with an <strong>SCP or bucket policy</strong> denying <code>s3:DeleteBucket</code>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>It shows you understand <strong>resource lifecycles</strong>: long-lived things (state, OIDC) are kept away from things you tear down often.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/bootstrap/state.tf","label":"bootstrap/state.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/provider.tf","label":"infra/provider.tf"}],"id":"s-your-terraform-state-bucket-was-deleted-14"},{"topic":"Terraform","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"ECR lives in the stack you destroy","q":"<p><code>destroy.yml</code> tears down <code>infra/</code>, and the ECR repository is in <code>infra/</code> with <code>force_delete = true</code>. What's the problem, and what would you change?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Destroying the environment <strong>deletes every image</strong>, including the SHAs you'd need for a rollback. After a rebuild, the first deploy has nothing to deploy until a new build runs.</li>\n<li>Move ECR into the <strong>bootstrap</strong> layer next to the state bucket and OIDC role, because it holds long-lived <strong>deployment artifacts</strong>. Then:<ul>\n<li>remove <code>force_delete</code> or set it to false,</li>\n<li>add an <strong>ECR lifecycle policy</strong> (e.g. keep the last 30 images) to control cost,</li>\n<li>pass the repository URL into <code>infra/</code> as a variable or data source.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>This is the improvement from your prep doc: it shows <strong>lifecycle and architecture thinking</strong>, not just Terraform syntax.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecr/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecr/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/destroy.yml","label":".github/workflows/destroy.yml"}],"id":"s-ecr-lives-in-the-stack-you-destroy-15"},{"topic":"AWS","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Logs from two weeks ago are gone","q":"<p>A user reports a problem from 14 days ago. You open CloudWatch Logs and there's nothing. Why?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>The log group <code>/ecs/memos</code> has <strong><code>retention_in_days = 7</code></strong>, so CloudWatch deletes events older than a week. That's a deliberate cost choice for a portfolio project.</li>\n<li>Options: raise retention (30 or 90 days), or <strong>export/subscribe</strong> logs to S3 with a lifecycle rule to Glacier for cheap long-term storage.</li>\n<li>For incidents you need more than logs: <strong>Container Insights</strong> is disabled (tfsec ignore, to save cost). Turning it on gives task-level CPU and memory metrics. Add <strong>alarms</strong> on ALB 5xx and unhealthy host count so you hear about problems before users do.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>It tests whether you know your <strong>retention trade-offs</strong>, and the difference between <strong>monitoring</strong> (alarms tell you <em>what</em>) and <strong>observability</strong> (logs, metrics and traces tell you <em>why</em>).</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"}],"id":"s-logs-from-two-weeks-ago-are-gone-16"},{"topic":"Security","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Why did you add tfsec ignores?","q":"<p>tfsec runs on every pipeline, but your code has several <code>tfsec:ignore</code> comments. Isn't that just hiding problems?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Each ignore is a <strong>documented, deliberate trade-off</strong> with a comment explaining it:<ul>\n<li><strong>VPC Flow Logs</strong> off to save cost. The accepted limit: no network flow records for investigations.</li>\n<li><strong>Container Insights</strong> off to save cost. Basic ECS metrics are enough for one task.</li>\n<li><strong>AWS-managed keys</strong> instead of customer KMS keys for ECR and CloudWatch, to avoid KMS charges.</li>\n<li><strong>Public ALB ingress on 80/443</strong> is the point of a public website.</li>\n<li><strong>ECS egress 443 to anywhere</strong> is needed for ECR, S3 and CloudWatch through the NAT gateway. VPC endpoints would let me narrow it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>For a real company I'd revisit the cost-based ones (Flow Logs and KMS especially) and track each ignore as a ticket.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Interviewers want to see that you <strong>make and document trade-offs</strong>, not that you silence a scanner. Having the reason in a comment next to the code is exactly that.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/vpc/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/vpc/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecr/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecr/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/security/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/security/main.tf"}],"id":"s-why-did-you-add-tfsec-ignores-17"},{"topic":"Terraform","level":"Senior","source":"Your Memos project","title":"Stand up Memos in a second region","q":"<p>The business wants the same platform running in a <strong>new AWS region</strong> (for example the UAE, me-central-1) alongside eu-west-1. How would you reuse this Terraform?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>The modules are already parameterised (<code>project_name</code>, <code>environment</code>, CIDRs, AZs, domain). Region-specific values go in a <strong>separate tfvars per environment/region</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Separate state per region</strong>: a different backend key, e.g. <code>uae/terraform.tfstate</code>, or a separate root module or workspace. One bad apply should never touch both regions.</li>\n<li>Things that change: <code>aws_region</code> and the AZ list, a <strong>non-overlapping VPC CIDR</strong> (e.g. 10.1.0.0/16) in case you ever peer, an <strong>ACM certificate in the new region</strong> (certificates are regional for ALBs), an <strong>ECR repository</strong> there (or ECR replication), and <strong>Route 53</strong> records (latency-based or geolocation routing, or a separate subdomain).</li>\n<li>Watch out for <strong>data residency rules</strong>: user data may have to stay in-country, so the database is per region, not shared.</li>\n<li>Pipeline: a matrix or per-region job, with the OIDC role trust and permissions extended to the new region.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>This matches the job you interviewed for (a new UAE region next to the UK platform). It shows that your code is reusable and that you think about <strong>state isolation and data residency</strong>.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/provider.tf","label":"infra/provider.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/terraform.tfvars","label":"infra/terraform.tfvars"}],"id":"s-stand-up-memos-in-a-second-region-18"},{"topic":"CI/CD","level":"Mid","source":"Your Memos project","title":"terraform apply runs with -auto-approve on main","q":"<p><code>deploy.yml</code> runs <code>terraform apply -auto-approve tfplan</code> with no human review. Is that safe? How would you add control?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>It applies the <strong>saved plan</strong> (<code>-out=tfplan</code>), so what's applied is exactly what was planned in that run. That part is good.</li>\n<li>But nobody reviews the plan before it runs. A change that destroys and recreates the ALB, or replaces the VPC, would go straight through.</li>\n<li>Add:<ol>\n<li><strong>Plan on pull request</strong>, with the plan output posted to the PR so a reviewer sees the real changes before merging.</li>\n<li>A <strong>GitHub environment <code>production</code></strong> with <strong>required reviewers</strong>. The apply job waits for approval, and the OIDC trust can require <code>environment:production</code>.</li>\n<li>A check that <strong>fails if the plan includes destroys</strong> of critical resources unless approved.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Keep <code>fmt -check</code>, <code>validate</code> and tfsec before plan, as now.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>It shows you can balance <strong>speed against safety</strong>, and it's a natural follow-up to OIDC least privilege.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml","label":".github/workflows/deploy.yml"}],"id":"s-terraform-apply-runs-with-auto-approve-o-19"},{"topic":"Troubleshooting","level":"Junior","source":"Your Memos project","title":"The task keeps getting killed: exit code 137","q":"<p>The Memos task stops every few hours with <strong>exit code 137</strong>. What does that mean, and what would you do?</p>","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>137 = 128 + 9</strong>: the process was killed with <strong>SIGKILL</strong>. On ECS this is almost always <strong>out of memory</strong>: the container went over its <strong>512 MB</strong> limit.</li>\n<li>Confirm: the stopped reason says <code>OutOfMemoryError</code>, and the service's memory metrics climb (Container Insights would show it per task).</li>\n<li>Fix: raise the task size. Fargate has fixed CPU/memory pairs, e.g. 256 CPU with 1024 MB, or 512 CPU with 1024–4096 MB. Find out <em>why</em> memory grows (a leak or a large import). Add a <strong>CloudWatch alarm on MemoryUtilization</strong>.</li>\n<li>Exit codes: <strong>0</strong> success · <strong>1</strong> app error · <strong>127</strong> command not found · <strong>137</strong> SIGKILL/OOM · <strong>143</strong> SIGTERM (a normal stop).</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Exit codes are quick wins in interviews, and tying 137 to your <strong>actual task size</strong> (0.25 vCPU, 512 MB) shows you know your own system.</p>"}],"links":[{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/main.tf","label":"infra/main.tf"},{"href":"https://github.com/EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra/blob/main/infra/modules/ecs/main.tf","label":"infra/modules/ecs/main.tf"}],"id":"s-the-task-keeps-getting-killed-exit-code--20"},{"topic":"Memos","kind":"story","source":"DevOps Revision Guide §14 · interview stories","q":"“Tell me about a problem you hit on a project and how you solved it.” <span class='hint-chip'>Story: OIDC wouldn’t authenticate (March 2026)</span>","answer":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> the pipeline’s AWS login step kept failing. The role couldn’t be assumed.","<b>What I tried:</b> a series of “Test OIDC” commits; downgrading <code>aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials</code> from v4 to v3 (that didn’t fix it); and <b>temporarily switching to static access keys</b> just to unblock the rest of the pipeline.","<b>Cause:</b> the role’s <b>trust policy</b> didn’t match the token GitHub sends. The <code>sub</code> claim has to match <code>repo:Owner/Repo:…</code> exactly, and mine didn’t (I’d renamed the repo).","<b>Fix:</b> recreated the OIDC provider and a fresh role with a correct trust policy, stored the role ARN as the <code>AWS_ROLE_ARN</code> secret, and switched back to OIDC. The trust policy now checks <code>aud = sts.amazonaws.com</code> (StringEquals) and <code>sub</code> like <code>repo:EnamulRahman/memos-aws-infra:*</code> (StringLike). Later it moved into the bootstrap stack as code.","<b>Lesson:</b> OIDC failures are nearly always the trust policy conditions. Use static keys only as a short-term unblock, then delete them. (Make sure the IAM access key really was deactivated and deleted, because an interviewer may ask.)","<b>Follow-up I’d make:</b> narrow <code>sub</code> to <code>ref:refs/heads/main</code>, and replace <code>AdministratorAccess</code> with least privilege."]}],"why":[{"type":"p","html":"Tell it as <b>Situation → Problem → How I found it → Fix → Lesson</b>. Stories 1, 2 and 6 are your strongest."}],"id":"s-tell-me-about-a-problem-you-hit-on-a-pro-21"},{"topic":"Memos","kind":"story","source":"DevOps Revision Guide §14 · interview stories","q":"“Tell me about a problem you hit on a project and how you solved it.” <span class='hint-chip'>Story:  .gitignore hid the program’s entry point</span>","answer":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> the image build failed because the main package <code>./cmd/memos</code> couldn’t be found, even though it was on my machine.","<b>Cause:</b> <code>app/.gitignore</code> contains the line <code>memos</code>, meant to ignore the <b>compiled binary</b>. A pattern without a slash matches <b>anywhere</b>, so it also ignored the folder <code>cmd/memos/</code>, and <code>main.go</code> was never committed.","<b>Fix:</b> added the folder explicitly (commit “Add memos cmd directory”). The correct pattern is <code>/memos</code>, which matches only at the top level.","<b>Lesson:</b> “works on my machine” can mean “not in Git”. Check <code>git status</code>, <code>git ls-files</code> and <code>git check-</code> <code>ignore -v &lt;path&gt;</code>."]}],"why":[{"type":"p","html":"Tell it as <b>Situation → Problem → How I found it → Fix → Lesson</b>. Stories 1, 2 and 6 are your strongest."}],"id":"s-tell-me-about-a-problem-you-hit-on-a-pro-22"},{"topic":"Memos","kind":"story","source":"DevOps Revision Guide §14 · interview stories","q":"“Tell me about a problem you hit on a project and how you solved it.” <span class='hint-chip'>Story: Go toolchain mismatch</span>","answer":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> the Go build stage failed.","<b>Cause:</b> the Dockerfile used <code>golang:1.23-alpine</code>, but the code’s <code>go.mod</code> needed a newer Go.","<b>Fix:</b> first added <code>ENV GOTOOLCHAIN=auto</code> so Go downloads the required version; the Dockerfile now uses <code>golang:1.25-alpine</code>, matching <code>go 1.25</code> in <code>go.mod</code>, so nothing extra is downloaded.","<b>Lesson:</b> match the build image to the code’s declared version. Don’t rely on downloads during a build."]}],"why":[{"type":"p","html":"Tell it as <b>Situation → Problem → How I found it → Fix → Lesson</b>. Stories 1, 2 and 6 are your strongest."}],"id":"s-tell-me-about-a-problem-you-hit-on-a-pro-23"},{"topic":"Memos","kind":"story","source":"DevOps Revision Guide §14 · interview stories","q":"“Tell me about a problem you hit on a project and how you solved it.” <span class='hint-chip'>Story: Port drift: 5230 vs 8081</span>","answer":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> the ALB showed targets as unhealthy and the site returned errors.","<b>Cause:</b> the port changed several times (Memos’s default 5230, then 8081 for the project spec), and for a while the Dockerfile, task definition, target group and security groups didn’t agree.","<b>Fix:</b> one port, <b>8081</b>, everywhere: <code>MEMOS_PORT</code>, <code>EXPOSE</code>, <code>container_port</code>, the target group port and health-check port, and both SG rules.","<b>Lesson:</b> a port lives in five places. Make it <b>one variable</b> (still to do: it’s hard-coded today)."]}],"why":[{"type":"p","html":"Tell it as <b>Situation → Problem → How I found it → Fix → Lesson</b>. Stories 1, 2 and 6 are your strongest."}],"id":"s-tell-me-about-a-problem-you-hit-on-a-pro-24"},{"topic":"Memos","kind":"story","source":"DevOps Revision Guide §14 · interview stories","q":"“Tell me about a problem you hit on a project and how you solved it.” <span class='hint-chip'>Story: CGO and C libraries</span>","answer":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> an earlier build needed C libraries in the runtime image (an early Dockerfile installed <code>libc6-</code> <code>compat</code> to run the binary).","<b>Fix:</b> build with <code>CGO_ENABLED=0</code>, giving a fully static Go binary. That works because Memos’s SQLite driver (<code>modernc.org/sqlite</code>) is pure Go. The runtime image now needs only <code>ca-certificates</code> and <code>tzdata</code>.","<b>Lesson:</b> a static binary makes the runtime image tiny and removes a class of “library not found” errors."]}],"why":[{"type":"p","html":"Tell it as <b>Situation → Problem → How I found it → Fix → Lesson</b>. Stories 1, 2 and 6 are your strongest."}],"id":"s-tell-me-about-a-problem-you-hit-on-a-pro-25"},{"topic":"Memos","kind":"story","source":"DevOps Revision Guide §14 · interview stories","q":"“Tell me about a problem you hit on a project and how you solved it.” <span class='hint-chip'>Story: Image size: three-stage build</span>","answer":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> a single-stage image carried Node, Go, compilers and all the source code.","<b>Fix:</b> three stages: frontend (Node) → backend (Go, with the built frontend embedded) → Alpine runtime with just the binary.","<b>Result (measured with</b> <code>docker images</code><b> , linux/amd64):</b> single-stage <b>2.79 GB</b> disk / 633 MB content → multi-stage <b>75.8 MB</b> / 19.7 MB. <b>About 97% smaller.</b> <code>Dockerfile.single-stage</code> is kept in the repo so anyone can reproduce it.","<b>Lesson:</b> smaller images pull faster, deploy faster, and have far less to attack."]}],"why":[{"type":"p","html":"Tell it as <b>Situation → Problem → How I found it → Fix → Lesson</b>. Stories 1, 2 and 6 are your strongest."}],"id":"s-tell-me-about-a-problem-you-hit-on-a-pro-26"},{"topic":"Memos","kind":"story","source":"DevOps Revision Guide §14 · interview stories","q":"“Tell me about a problem you hit on a project and how you solved it.” <span class='hint-chip'>Story: Immutable tags broke pipeline re-runs</span>","answer":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["<b>Problem:</b> after turning on <b>immutable tags</b> in ECR, re-running a build for the same commit failed: you can’t push the same tag twice.","<b>Fix:</b> the build step now calls <code>aws ecr describe-images</code> first. If the SHA tag exists, it <b>reuses</b> the image; if the error is <code>ImageNotFoundException</code>, it builds and pushes; any other error fails the job.","<b>Lesson:</b> make pipelines <b>idempotent</b>, so re-running the same commit is safe."]}],"why":[{"type":"p","html":"Tell it as <b>Situation → Problem → How I found it → Fix → Lesson</b>. Stories 1, 2 and 6 are your strongest."}],"id":"s-tell-me-about-a-problem-you-hit-on-a-pro-27"},{"topic":"Memos","kind":"story","source":"DevOps Revision Guide §14 · interview stories","q":"“Tell me about a problem you hit on a project and how you solved it.” <span class='hint-chip'>Story: Restructuring: bootstrap, native locking and imports (October 2026)</span>","answer":[{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":["<b>Change:</b> moved the state bucket and the OIDC provider and role into a separate <b>bootstrap</b> stack, and switched the backend to <b>S3 native locking</b> (<code>use_lockfile = true</code>, no DynamoDB).","<b>Problem:</b> the ACM DNS validation records already existed in Route 53. Terraform wanted to create them again and would fail.","<b>Fix:</b> added <code>import</code> <b>blocks</b> for <code>module.acm.aws_route53_record.validation[\"myegh.com\"]</code> and <code>[\"tm.myegh.com\"]</code>, ran plan/apply until there were no changes, then deleted <code>imports.tf</code>.","<b>Lesson:</b> use import to adopt real resources instead of recreating them, and keep the resources that must outlive a destroy in their own stack.","<b>Persistent data:</b> RDS PostgreSQL (or EFS), with secrets in Secrets Manager.","<b>High availability:</b> 2+ tasks across AZs, plus Service Auto Scaling.","<b>Least-privilege OIDC role,</b> and restrict <code>sub</code> to the main branch or an environment.","<b>Health check path →</b> <code>/healthz</code><b> ,</b> plus the ECS deployment circuit breaker with automatic rollback.","<b>Pipeline gates:</b> Go tests, Trivy image scan, <code>plan</code> on PRs, a production approval environment.","<b>Clean-up:</b> one port variable, delete or use the route53 module, fix the README, use conventional commit messages."]}],"why":[{"type":"p","html":"Tell it as <b>Situation → Problem → How I found it → Fix → Lesson</b>. Stories 1, 2 and 6 are your strongest."}],"id":"s-tell-me-about-a-problem-you-hit-on-a-pro-28"},{"topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault · Linux","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"Explain the linux boot process (detailed)","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>\n<p>BIOS/UEFI: When you power on the computer, the Basic Input/Output System (BIOS) or Unified Extensible Firmware Interface (UEFI) firmware is invoked. It performs hardware initialization, self-tests, and determines the boot device.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Bootloader: GRUB (the most common Linux bootloader) loads the operating system into memory.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Kernel Initialization: Once the bootloader hands off control, the Linux kernel is loaded into memory. It starts executing from its entry point. The kernel is responsible for managing system resources, such as memory, processes, devices, and file systems.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Init Process: The first user-space process, known as the init process, is started by the kernel. The init process has a process ID (PID) of 1 and is responsible for initializing the system further. In modern Linux distributions that use systemd as the init system, the init process is replaced by the systemd process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Init System/Services: System services and daemons are launched.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Login Manager: A login screen or prompt appears for user authentication.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>User Session: After login, the user's session starts with the desktop environment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In summary, the boot process involves firmware initialization, bootloader loading the OS, kernel taking control, system initialization, login prompt, and user session start.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-explain-the-linux-boot-process-detailed-29"},{"topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault · Linux","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"A process on the system can no longer log files, how would you debug?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Check File Permissions</strong>: Verify that the process has proper permissions to write to the log files or directories. Ensure that the file permissions and ownership are correctly set to allow the process to write logs.</li>\n<li><strong>Review Log File Configuration</strong>: Check the configuration file (e.g., /etc/rsyslog.conf or /etc/syslog-ng/syslog-ng.conf) to ensure that the log files and their destinations are correctly defined.</li>\n<li><strong>Check Disk Space</strong>: Insufficient disk space can prevent file logging. Use the df command to check the available disk space. Ensure that the filesystem containing the log files has enough free space to accommodate new logs.</li>\n<li><strong>Check System Logs</strong>: Review the system logs (/var/log/syslog, /var/log/messages, etc.) for any relevant error messages or indications of issues related to the logging process. Look for log entries related to the process and check if any error messages are reported.</li>\n<li><strong>Restart the Logging Service</strong>: Restart the logging service associated with the process (if you use rsyslog for logging) &gt;&gt; <code>sudo systemctl restart rsyslog</code></li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-a-process-on-the-system-can-no-longer-lo-30"},{"topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault · Linux","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"How can I check if a Linux system is healthy?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>System Resource Usage</strong>: </li>\n<li>Monitor CPU usage: Use tools like <code>top</code> or <code>htop</code> to check CPU usage and identify any processes consuming excessive CPU resources.</li>\n<li>Check memory usage: Use commands like <code>free</code> or <code>top</code> to examine memory usage and ensure sufficient free memory is available.</li>\n<li>\n<p>Monitor disk usage: Use <code>df</code> or <code>du</code> commands to check disk space utilization and identify any partitions nearing capacity limits.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>System Services and Processes:</strong>: </p>\n</li>\n<li>Check running processes: Use <code>ps</code> or <code>top</code> to view running processes and ensure critical services are active.</li>\n<li>\n<p>Verify system services: Use service management tools like <code>systemctl</code> (systemd) or <code>service</code> (init) to check the status of essential services.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>System Logs</strong>: </p>\n</li>\n<li>Review system logs: Examine log files in <code>/var/log/</code> (e.g., <code>/var/log/syslog</code>, <code>/var/log/messages</code>) for any error or warning messages related to system components, applications, or hardware.</li>\n<li>\n<p>Monitor log files in real-time: Use the <code>tail</code> command with the -f option to track log files as new entries are added.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Network Connectivity</strong>: </p>\n</li>\n<li>Check network interfaces: Use <code>ifconfig</code> or <code>ip</code> command to verify the status and configuration of network interfaces.</li>\n<li>\n<p>Test network connectivity: Use <code>ping</code> or <code>traceroute</code> to test connectivity to remote hosts or check for network latency or packet loss.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Hardware monitoring?</p>\n</li>\n<li>Security and updates?</li>\n<li>Backup and recover?</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-how-can-i-check-if-a-linux-system-is-hea-31"},{"topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault · Linux","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"What happens when you type &quot;ls&quot; or &quot;cd&quot; into a terminal? (go deep and talk about what happens behind the scenes - kernel level)","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The terminal program receives the command you typed and identifies the command and any arguments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The shell then searches for the location of the command binary within the directories specified in the <code>PATH</code> environment variable. It looks for an executable file with a matching name.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Once the command binary is located, the shell initiates a system call, specifically the <code>execve()</code> system call, to load the command into memory and execute it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The kernel allocates memory for the command and sets up file descriptors for input, output, and error handling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The kernel performs a context switch, transitioning from the shell to the command.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The command binary is loaded into memory, and its execution begins.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>As the command executes, it may make additional system calls to interact with the kernel. For example, the \"ls\" command might make system calls to read directory contents or retrieve file metadata.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The command may manipulate the terminal's display using control codes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Once the command completes, the kernel returns control to the shell.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-what-happens-when-you-type-ls-or-cd-into-32"},{"topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault · Linux","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"How can I check if a server is down?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>The ping command is a simple and widely used tool to check the connectivity between your Linux system and a remote server &gt;&gt; <code>ping &lt;server_address&gt;</code></li>\n<li>The telnet command allows you to establish a connection to a specific port on a server. By attempting to connect to a server's port, you can determine if it's up and accepting connections &gt;&gt; <code>telnet &lt;server_address&gt; &lt;port&gt;</code></li>\n<li>If the telnet command is not available on your system, you can use nc (netcat), which provides similar functionality &gt;&gt; <code>nc -zv &lt;server_address&gt; &lt;port&gt;</code></li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-how-can-i-check-if-a-server-is-down-33"},{"topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault · Linux","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"How are Linux processes killed on a lower level?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>\n<p>In Linux, processes can be terminated or killed at a lower level using signals. Signals are software interrupts sent to a process to convey various notifications or requests. They can be used to terminate a process gracefully, forcefully, or perform other actions</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SIGTERM (Signal 15): This is the default termination signal sent to a process when you use the kill command without specifying a signal. It politely requests the process to terminate and allows it to perform cleanup operations before exiting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SIGKILL (Signal 9): This signal forcefully terminates a process. It does not allow the process to perform any cleanup or graceful shutdown procedures. The process is immediately terminated.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SIGINT (Signal 2): This signal is generated when you press Ctrl+C on the keyboard. It is typically used to interrupt or terminate a process that is running in the foreground.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>SIGQUIT (Signal 3): Similar to SIGINT, this signal is generated when you press Ctrl+\\ on the keyboard. It usually requests a process to terminate and provides a core dump for debugging purposes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-how-are-linux-processes-killed-on-a-lowe-34"},{"topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault · Linux","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"I have accidentally entered `cd/bin` and done `chmod 644 chmod` - how can I fix this?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Method 1 - Copy the file from another system</strong>:\n- If you have compatible systems, you can always just grab a copy of chmod from another server using scp or rsync.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sh\">cd /bin\nmv chmod chmod.orig\nscp twin:/bin/chmod .\ndiff chmod chmod.orig</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Method 2 - Restoring from Backup</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If you have a recent backup of the affected system, you can restore the correct permissions of the <code>chmod</code> command by replacing it with the version from the backup. Copy the <code>chmod</code> binary from the backup location to the <code>/bin</code> directory, ensuring that the correct permissions are retained.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Method 3</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-i-have-accidentally-entered-cd-bin-and-d-35"},{"topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault · Linux","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"How would you troubleshoot a network connectivity issue in Linux?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Check Network Configuration</strong>: Verify that the network configuration is correct. Use commands like <code>ifconfig</code> or <code>ip</code> to check the status of network interfaces, IP addresses, subnet masks, and gateway settings.</li>\n<li><strong>Ping</strong>: Use the ping command to test basic connectivity to a remote host or IP address. For example, <code>ping 8.8.8.8</code> pings Google's public DNS server. If the ping is successful, it indicates that network connectivity is established.</li>\n<li><strong>Check DNS Resolution</strong>: If you are having trouble accessing websites by domain name, check if DNS resolution is functioning correctly. Use the <code>nslookup</code> or <code>dig</code> command to query DNS servers and verify if they are returning the correct IP addresses for the given domain</li>\n<li><strong>Check Routing</strong>: Use the <code>traceroute</code> or <code>tracepath</code> command to trace the route packets take to reach a destination. It helps identify any network hops or routing issues along the path.</li>\n<li><strong>Firewall Configuration</strong>: Review the firewall configuration to ensure it is not blocking the required network traffic. Check the rules in tools like <code>iptables</code>, <code>ufw</code>, or other firewall management tools.</li>\n<li><strong>Check Network Services</strong>: Ensure that required network services, such as DHCP (Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol) or DNS, are running and functioning properly. Use commands like <code>systemctl</code> or <code>service</code> to check the status of specific services.</li>\n<li><strong>Inspect Network Logs</strong>: Check system logs in <code>/var/log/</code> (e.g., <code>/var/log/syslog</code>, <code>/var/log/messages</code>) for any relevant error messages related to network interfaces, services, or connectivity issues.</li>\n<li><strong>Test Connectivity with Different Tools</strong>: Use network troubleshooting tools like <code>netcat</code> (nc), <code>curl</code>, or <code>wget</code> to test connectivity to specific ports or services on remote hosts.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-how-would-you-troubleshoot-a-network-con-36"},{"topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault · Linux","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"How do you troubleshoot a connectivity issue with a remote server in Linux?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>Check Network Connectivity &gt;&gt; <code>ping &lt;server_ip_or_hostname&gt;</code></li>\n<li>Verify DNS Resolution &gt;&gt; <code>nslookup &lt;server_hostname&gt;</code> or <code>dig</code></li>\n<li>Check Firewall Settings: Verify that your local firewall or any intermediate firewalls are not blocking the necessary network traffic. Temporarily disable the firewall or configure it to allow the specific ports or protocols required to communicate with the remote server.</li>\n<li>Verify Port Connectivity &gt;&gt; <code>telnet &lt;server_ip_or_hostname&gt; &lt;port_number&gt;</code></li>\n<li>Trace Route: Use the traceroute or tracepath command to trace the network path to the remote server &gt;&gt; <code>traceroute &lt;server_ip_or_hostname&gt;</code></li>\n<li>Review Server Logs: Examine the server's logs for any error messages or indications of connectivity issues. Check logs related to the specific services running on the server, such as Apache logs for web server issues.</li>\n<li>Check Server Services: Ensure that the necessary services on the remote server are running and properly configured</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-how-do-you-troubleshoot-a-connectivity-i-37"},{"topic":"Linux","source":"tech-vault · Linux","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"How do you view and edit the system logs in Linux?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>View logs &gt;&gt; <code>cat /var/log/syslog</code> - use SUDO</li>\n<li>Edit logs &gt;&gt; <code>vim /var/log/syslog</code> - use SUDO</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-how-do-you-view-and-edit-the-system-logs-38"},{"topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault · Networking","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"When I type google.com into the browser, what actually happens? (basic version)","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When you type \"google.com\" into your web browser's address bar and hit Enter, several steps occur behind the scenes to display the Google homepage. Here's a simple breakdown of what happens:</p>\n<p><strong>DNS Resolution:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your web browser first checks its local cache to see if it already knows the IP address associated with \"google.com.\" If it does, it skips the next steps.</li>\n<li>If not found in the cache, your browser sends a DNS (Domain Name System) resolution request to a DNS resolver. This is usually provided by your internet service provider (ISP) or a public DNS service like Google's 8.8.8.8.</li>\n<li>The DNS resolver checks its cache to see if it has the IP address for \"google.com.\" If not, it initiates the DNS resolution process.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>DNS Resolution Process:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The DNS resolver queries root DNS servers to find the authoritative DNS server for the top-level domain (TLD), which is \"com\" in this case.</li>\n<li>The root DNS server responds with the IP address of the authoritative DNS server for the \"com\" TLD.</li>\n<li>The resolver then queries the authoritative DNS server for the \"com\" TLD to find the authoritative DNS server for \"google.com.\"</li>\n<li>The authoritative DNS server for \"google.com\" responds with the IP address associated with \"google.com.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IP Address Retrieval:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The DNS resolver returns the IP address of \"google.com\" to your web browser.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>HTTP Request:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your browser initiates an HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) request to the IP address it received for \"google.com.\"</li>\n<li>The request includes additional information like the specific web page you're trying to access (e.g., \"/\", which typically represents the homepage).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Server Processing:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Google's web server, located at the provided IP address, receives the HTTP request.</li>\n<li>The server processes the request and generates an appropriate response.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Response Transmission:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The web server sends back an HTTP response, which includes the HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and other resources needed to render the Google homepage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rendering the Web Page:</strong></p>\n<p>Your browser receives the HTTP response and begins rendering the Google homepage on your screen.\nIt may also make additional requests for images, stylesheets, and scripts referenced in the HTML to fully load and display the page.</p>\n<p><strong>Page Display:</strong></p>\n<p>Once all resources are loaded, your web browser displays the Google homepage on your screen, and you can interact with it.\nIn summary, when you type \"google.com\" into your browser, a complex series of steps involving DNS resolution, HTTP requests, and server responses occurs to retrieve and display the webpage you requested. This process ensures that you can access websites using human-readable domain names while the internet works behind the scenes using IP addresses to route and deliver content.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-when-i-type-google-com-into-the-browser--39"},{"topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault · Networking","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"I can&#x27;t reach a website, how can I troubleshoot? (use deep Linux + networking knowledge)","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Check Network Connectivity:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Ensure your Linux machine has a working internet connection. Try pinging a reliable website or an IP address (e.g., <code>ping google.com</code> or <code>ping 8.8.8.8</code> for Google's DNS).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>DNS Resolution:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Verify that DNS resolution is working correctly. Try to resolve the website's IP address using <code>nslookup</code> or <code>dig</code> (e.g., <code>nslookup website.com</code> or <code>dig website.com</code>).</li>\n<li>Check your DNS server configuration in /etc/resolv.conf. Ensure it points to a valid DNS server (e.g., <code>nameserver 8.8.8.8</code> for Google's DNS).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Check Hosts File:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Make sure the website is not blocked by entries in the <code>/etc/hosts</code> file. Check for any entries that may be redirecting the website.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Firewall Rules:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Check if a firewall is blocking outgoing or incoming traffic. Use the <code>iptables</code> or firewall-cmd command to inspect firewall rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Web Proxy:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If you are behind a proxy server, ensure that proxy settings are correctly configured in your environment, including <code>HTTP_PROXY</code> and <code>HTTPS_PROXY</code> environment variables.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Browser Issues:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Try accessing the website from a different web browser or an incognito/private browsing window to rule out browser-related problems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>SSL/TLS Issues:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If you are getting SSL/TLS-related errors, ensure your system has up-to-date CA certificates installed. You can also check for SSL/TLS issues using the openssl command (e.g., <code>openssl s_client -connect website.com:443</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Check DNS Cache:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If you've made changes to DNS configurations, clear your DNS cache using <code>systemctl restart systemd-resolved</code> (on systems using systemd-resolved) or <code>sudo /etc/init.d/nscd restart</code> (on systems using nscd).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trace Route (Traceroute):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the traceroute or tracepath command to trace the network path to the website (e.g., <code>traceroute website.com</code> or <code>tracepath website.com</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Check System Logs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inspect system logs in <code>/var/log</code> (e.g., <code>/var/log/syslog</code>, <code>/var/log/messages</code>, <code>/var/log/auth.log</code>) for any network-related errors or issues.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Check DNS Server Status:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Verify the availability and responsiveness of your DNS servers by pinging them or using tools like <code>dig</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Check Website Status:</strong></p>\n<p>It's possible that the website itself is down. Check its status on a service like \"DownDetector\" or \"Is It Down Right Now?\".</p>\n<p><strong>Packet Capture (Optional):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>As a last resort, you can use packet capture tools like <code>tcpdump</code> or <code>wireshark</code> to capture network traffic and analyze it for issues.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-i-can-t-reach-a-website-how-can-i-troubl-40"},{"topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault · Networking","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"Can you break down the OSI model and what does it signify?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) model is a conceptual framework that standardizes and defines the functions of a telecommunication or networking system. It consists of seven distinct layers, each responsible for specific tasks in the communication process. Here's a breakdown of the OSI model and what each layer signifies:</p>\n<p><strong>Physical Layer (Layer 1):</strong></p>\n<p>Significance: This layer deals with the physical medium and transmission of raw binary data over a physical network.\nResponsibilities: It defines the physical characteristics of the network, such as cables, connectors, electrical voltages, and transmission speeds. It also specifies how bits are represented, encoded, and transmitted over the network medium.</p>\n<p><strong>Data Link Layer (Layer 2):</strong></p>\n<p>Significance: This layer focuses on reliable data transfer between directly connected devices on a local network.\nResponsibilities: It manages the framing of data into frames, error detection and correction, and flow control. Ethernet and Wi-Fi operate at this layer. MAC (Media Access Control) addresses are used for device identification.</p>\n<p><strong>Network Layer (Layer 3):</strong></p>\n<p>Significance: The network layer is responsible for routing packets across different networks to reach their destination.\nResponsibilities: It handles logical addressing, routing, and packet forwarding. The Internet Protocol (IP) operates at this layer, and IP addresses are used to identify devices on different networks.</p>\n<p><strong>Transport Layer (Layer 4):</strong></p>\n<p>Significance: This layer ensures end-to-end communication and data reliability between devices on different networks.\nResponsibilities: It manages data segmentation, flow control, error detection, and retransmission. TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) and UDP (User Datagram Protocol) are commonly used transport layer protocols.</p>\n<p><strong>Session Layer (Layer 5):</strong></p>\n<p>Significance: The session layer establishes, manages, and terminates communication sessions between two devices.\nResponsibilities: It handles session setup, maintenance, and teardown, including synchronization, checkpointing, and recovery of data in case of interruptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Presentation Layer (Layer 6):</strong></p>\n<p>Significance: This layer ensures that data is presented in a readable format for the application layer.\nResponsibilities: It handles data translation, encryption, compression, and character encoding to ensure that data sent by one device can be understood by another.</p>\n<p><strong>Application Layer (Layer 7):</strong></p>\n<p>Significance: The application layer is the top layer and represents the actual software applications that users interact with.\nResponsibilities: It provides a platform-independent interface for application software to communicate with the lower layers. This layer includes protocols like HTTP, FTP, SMTP, and DNS, which enable specific application-level functions.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-can-you-break-down-the-osi-model-and-wha-41"},{"topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault · Networking","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"How does mTLS work and compare it to TLS?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>mTLS (mutual Transport Layer Security) is an extension of the traditional TLS (Transport Layer Security) protocol. Both TLS and mTLS are cryptographic protocols used to secure data transmission over a network, but they differ in how they authenticate and secure the communication.</p>\n<p><strong>TLS (Transport Layer Security):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>TLS is used to establish secure, encrypted communication between a client (such as a web browser) and a server (such as a web server).</li>\n<li>In a typical TLS handshake, the server presents its digital certificate to prove its identity to the client.</li>\n<li>The client verifies the server's certificate against a trusted certificate authority (CA) to ensure it's valid.</li>\n<li>Once the server's identity is verified, a symmetric encryption key is exchanged, and encrypted communication begins.</li>\n<li>TLS is primarily used for server authentication and encryption of data in transit between a client and a server.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>mTLS (Mutual Transport Layer Security):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>mTLS, also known as two-way TLS or client-side TLS, extends TLS by requiring both the client and the server to present digital certificates and authenticate each other.</li>\n<li>In an mTLS handshake, the client and server exchange certificates and verify each other's identities.</li>\n<li>The client verifies the server's certificate, just like in traditional TLS.</li>\n<li>The server, in turn, verifies the client's certificate to ensure it's valid and belongs to an authorized client.</li>\n<li>Once both parties are authenticated, a symmetric encryption key is exchanged, and encrypted communication begins.</li>\n<li>mTLS is used when both parties (client and server) need to be authenticated and data encryption is required. It's often used in scenarios like secure API communication and IoT device authentication.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In summary, TLS and mTLS are both cryptographic protocols for securing data transmission, but mTLS goes a step further by requiring mutual authentication, where both the client and server authenticate each other using digital certificates. This added layer of security is useful in scenarios where bidirectional trust is essential, such as in securing APIs, web services, or IoT device communication.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-how-does-mtls-work-and-compare-it-to-tls-42"},{"topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault · Networking","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"Describe the TCP/IP connection process?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>The TCP/IP (Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol) connection process involves several steps that occur when two devices communicate over a network. Key steps include:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Initialization:</strong></p>\n<p>The client initiates the connection by sending a TCP segment with the SYN (synchronize) flag set to the server.\nThis initiates the \"three-way handshake\" process.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Three-Way Handshake:</strong></p>\n<p>SYN (Synchronize): The client sends a TCP segment with the SYN flag set, indicating its intent to establish a connection. It also selects an initial sequence number.\nSYN-ACK (Synchronize-Acknowledgment): The server responds with a TCP segment that has the SYN and ACK (acknowledge) flags set. It acknowledges the client's sequence number and selects its own initial sequence number.\nACK (Acknowledgment): The client sends an acknowledgment back to the server, acknowledging the server's sequence number. At this point, the connection is established, and both parties are synchronized.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Data Transfer:</strong></p>\n<p>Once the connection is established, data can be transmitted in both directions.\nData is divided into segments, each with a sequence number for tracking and reordering.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Termination:</strong></p>\n<p>When one party wishes to close the connection, it sends a TCP segment with the FIN (finish) flag set.\nThe other party acknowledges the FIN and may also send its own FIN.\nThis initiates a \"four-way handshake\" to ensure that both sides agree to close the connection gracefully.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Four-Way Handshake (Connection Termination):</strong></p>\n<p>FIN (Finish): One party sends a TCP segment with the FIN flag set to indicate the intent to close the connection.\nACK (Acknowledgment): The other party acknowledges the FIN.\nFIN: The acknowledging party may also send its own FIN to close the connection from its side.\nACK: The initial party acknowledges the FIN from the other side, completing the termination process.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Connection Closed:</strong></p>\n<p>After the four-way handshake, the connection is closed, and no further data can be exchanged.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-describe-the-tcp-ip-connection-process-43"},{"topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault · Networking","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"When and why would one use a TCP over UDP?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) and UDP (User Datagram Protocol) are two transport layer protocols used in networking, and each has its own strengths and weaknesses. The choice between TCP and UDP depends on the specific requirements of the application or service being used. Here's when and why you might choose TCP over UDP:</p>\n<p><strong>Use TCP When:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Reliability is Critical: TCP provides a reliable and error-checked connection. It ensures that data is delivered accurately and in the correct order. This makes it suitable for applications where data integrity is crucial, such as file transfers, email, and web browsing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ordered Data Delivery: If your application requires data to be delivered in the same order it was sent, TCP is the better choice. It maintains a sequencing mechanism to guarantee ordered data delivery.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Flow Control: TCP includes flow control mechanisms to prevent overwhelming the receiver with too much data too quickly. This is essential for applications that require rate limiting, like streaming video or real-time communication apps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Error Recovery: TCP can detect and recover from errors that may occur during transmission. It retransmits lost or corrupted packets to ensure data reliability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Connection-Oriented: TCP establishes a connection before data transfer begins and releases it after the transfer is complete. This connection-oriented nature is suitable for applications that require a reliable, well-defined start and end to communication sessions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Use UDP When:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Low Latency is Critical: UDP is faster than TCP due to its minimal overhead. It's suitable for applications that prioritize low latency, such as online gaming, live streaming, and VoIP (Voice over IP).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Loss Tolerance: UDP does not guarantee data delivery or order, so it's a good choice for applications where occasional packet loss is acceptable, such as real-time data feeds, video conferencing, and some IoT applications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Reduced Network Overhead: UDP has less overhead than TCP because it doesn't involve establishing connections, maintaining sequencing, or performing extensive error checking. This can be advantageous for applications with limited bandwidth or high data rates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Broadcast or Multicast: UDP supports broadcast and multicast communication, which allows data to be sent to multiple recipients simultaneously. This is useful for applications like online gaming and video streaming.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Simple Implementation: UDP is easier to implement because it doesn't have the complexities of connection establishment and error recovery. This makes it a good choice for lightweight applications and protocols.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In summary, use TCP when data reliability, ordered delivery, and error recovery are critical. Choose UDP when low latency, reduced overhead, and loss tolerance are more important, and you can handle potential data loss or out-of-order delivery at the application level. The decision depends on the specific needs of your application and the trade-offs you are willing to make between reliability and performance.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-when-and-why-would-one-use-a-tcp-over-ud-44"},{"topic":"Networking","source":"tech-vault · Networking","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"Data transfer between 2 hosts is extremely slow. How can you troubleshoot?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><strong>Check Network Connectivity:</strong></p>\n<p>Verify that both hosts have a stable and functioning network connection. Ensure that cables, switches, and routers are working correctly.</p>\n<p><strong>Test Other Network Services:</strong></p>\n<p>Determine if the slowness is specific to data transfer or if it affects other network services as well. Try accessing websites, pinging other hosts, or testing other network applications to see if the issue is widespread or isolated.</p>\n<p><strong>Check Host Resource Utilization:</strong></p>\n<p>Monitor the CPU, memory, and disk utilization on both hosts. High resource usage can lead to slow data transfer. Identify any resource-intensive processes or applications that may be affecting performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Review Network Configuration:</strong></p>\n<p>Verify that the network configurations on both hosts are correct, including IP addresses, subnet masks, gateways, and DNS settings. Ensure there are no conflicts or misconfigurations.</p>\n<p><strong>Check for Network Congestion:</strong></p>\n<p>Network congestion can slow down data transfer. Monitor network traffic and identify any bottlenecks or excessive usage on the network segments between the two hosts.</p>\n<p><strong>Test Connectivity to Other Hosts:</strong></p>\n<p>Try transferring data between each host and other devices on the network to determine if the issue is specific to the connection between these two hosts.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-data-transfer-between-2-hosts-is-extreme-45"},{"topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault · AWS","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"I want to create a 3 Tier Architecture. Can you explain step by step of how I can go about this?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Presentation Layer (Web):</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Step 1: Launch an Amazon EC2 instance for your web server. This instance will host your website or web application.\nStep 2: Configure security groups to allow incoming web traffic (HTTP/HTTPS) to the web server.\nStep 3: Optionally, attach an Elastic IP (EIP) for a static public IP address.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Application Layer (App):</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Step 4: Launch another EC2 instance for your application server(s). This instance runs the business logic of your application.\nStep 5: Configure security groups to allow incoming traffic from the web server to the application server on the necessary ports.\nStep 6: Install and configure any middleware or application software required for your application.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Data Layer (Database):</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Step 7: Create an Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service) instance or set up a database server (e.g., on EC2) for your database layer.\nStep 8: Configure security groups to allow incoming database connections only from the application server(s).\nStep 9: Create your database schema and tables, and configure database access and permissions.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-i-want-to-create-a-3-tier-architecture-c-46"},{"topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault · AWS","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"In VPC with private and public subnets, database servers should ideally be launched into which subnet?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>In a VPC with both private and public subnets, database servers should ideally be launched into the <strong>private subnets</strong>. Placing database servers in private subnets ensures that they are not directly accessible from the internet, which enhances security. Public subnets are typically used for resources that need to be accessible from the internet, like web servers.</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-in-vpc-with-private-and-public-subnets-d-47"},{"topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault · AWS","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"What are some security best practices for EC2s?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use Security Groups</strong>: Configure security groups to control incoming and outgoing traffic to your EC2 instances. Only allow necessary ports and sources.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use Key Pairs</strong>: Authenticate access to your EC2 instances using key pairs (SSH keys for Linux or RDP keys for Windows) instead of passwords.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Regular Updates</strong>: Keep your EC2 instances and their software up to date with security patches and updates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use IAM Roles</strong>: Assign IAM roles to EC2 instances to grant them specific permissions, reducing the need for storing access keys on the instance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Enable Monitoring</strong>: Use Amazon CloudWatch to monitor performance and set up alarms for unusual activity or resource exhaustion.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Data Encryption</strong>: Encrypt data at rest using services like Amazon EBS encryption and in-transit using SSL/TLS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Snapshot Backups</strong>: Create EBS snapshots and AMIs regularly to back up your instances, ensuring data recovery in case of failures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Instance Isolation</strong>: Separate instances into public and private subnets within a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) for network isolation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Disable Root Access</strong>: Disable remote root login on Linux instances and use a sudo user for administrative tasks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IAM Policies</strong>: Use fine-grained IAM policies to restrict access based on the principle of least privilege.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use Bastion Hosts</strong>: If necessary, employ a bastion host (jump server) to access private instances securely.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Monitor Access</strong>: Monitor and audit access to your EC2 instances, including login attempts and user actions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Automate Security</strong>: Implement automation tools like AWS Systems Manager and AWS Config to enforce security policies.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-what-are-some-security-best-practices-fo-48"},{"topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault · AWS","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"I created a web application with autoscaling. I observed that the traffic on my application is the highest on Wednesdays and Fridays between 9 AM and 7 PM. What would be the best solution for me to handle the scaling?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>To handle scaling for your web application with high traffic on Wednesdays and Fridays between 9 AM and 7 PM, you can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Schedule-Based Auto Scaling</strong>: Use AWS Auto Scaling's scheduled scaling feature. Set up a schedule to increase the desired number of instances during the peak hours on Wednesdays and Fridays and decrease it during off-peak hours.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Target Tracking Scaling Policy</strong>: Create a target tracking scaling policy based on a metric like CPU utilization or request count per instance. Set the target to automatically adjust the number of instances to maintain a specific metric value. This can help handle traffic fluctuations efficiently.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By combining these approaches, your application can automatically adjust its capacity to meet demand, ensuring it scales up during peak times and scales down during quieter periods, optimizing cost and performance.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-i-created-a-web-application-with-autosca-49"},{"topic":"AWS","source":"tech-vault · AWS","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"You have an application running on your Amazon EC2 instance. You want to reduce the load on your instance as soon as the CPU utilization reaches 100 percent. How will you do that?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p><br></p>\n<p>To automatically reduce the load on your Amazon EC2 instance when CPU utilization reaches 100 percent, follow these steps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Create an Auto Scaling Group</strong>: Set up an Auto Scaling Group for your EC2 instance if you haven't already.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Create a Target Tracking Scaling Policy</strong>: Within the Auto Scaling Group configuration, create a Target Tracking Scaling Policy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Select CPU Utilization as the Metric</strong>: Choose CPU utilization as the metric to track.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Set the Target Value</strong>: Specify the target CPU utilization level at which you want to take action, such as 80 or 90 percent, not 100 percent, to avoid any performance issues. AWS Auto Scaling will automatically adjust the number of instances to maintain this target value.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Define Scaling Actions</strong>: Configure scaling actions for when the CPU utilization exceeds the target value. You can specify actions to increase or decrease the number of instances based on your requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Apply the Policy</strong>: Apply the Target Tracking Scaling Policy to your Auto Scaling Group.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-you-have-an-application-running-on-your--50"},{"topic":"Kubernetes","source":"tech-vault · Kubernetes","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"What happens when a master node fails?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>If a master node fails in Kubernetes, the cluster’s control plane becomes unavailable, meaning no new Pods can be scheduled, and management operations cannot be processed. However, existing workloads on worker nodes continue to run. To prevent downtime, clusters often have high-availability setups with multiple master nodes so that if one fails, others can take over.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-what-happens-when-a-master-node-fails-51"},{"topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault · CI/CD","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"What happens if a pipeline fails?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When a pipeline fails, execution stops immediately and the failure is reported. This prevents invalid or unstable code from progressing to later stages such as deployment.</p>\n<p>Failures are logged with clear output, allowing developers to identify issues such as test failures, syntax errors, misconfigured environment variables, or broken dependencies. Early failure is intentional and protects production systems while encouraging fast correction.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-what-happens-if-a-pipeline-fails-52"},{"topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault · CI/CD","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"Walk me through a CI/CD pipeline, from code commit to production?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>From code commit to production, the CI/CD pipeline starts when a developer pushes code to the remote repository or opens a pull request, which automatically triggers the continuous integration (CI) process.</p>\n<p>The pipeline first runs in the development environment, where the code is checked out, dependencies are resolved, and the application is built. Static checks such as linting and formatting are executed, followed by fast-running unit tests to validate the code in a development context.</p>\n<p>If this stage succeeds, the pipeline packages the application into a versioned artefact, such as a binary or container image, and stores it in an artefact repository. This artefact is built once and then promoted unchanged through subsequent environments.</p>\n<p>Next, as part of continuous delivery (CD), the artefact is deployed to the staging environment, which mirrors production as closely as possible. In staging, integration tests, smoke tests, and health checks are run to verify system behaviour and interactions with external services. Deployment strategies such as rolling or blue–green deployments are commonly used to reduce risk.</p>\n<p>After successful validation in staging, and often following a manual approval step, the same artefact is deployed to the production environment. The deployment is monitored using logs, metrics, and alerts, and automated rollback mechanisms are in place if any issues are detected.</p>\n<p>This pipeline enforces a clear separation between development, staging, and production environments while ensuring consistency, traceability, and a controlled release process from code commit to production.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-walk-me-through-a-ci-cd-pipeline-from-co-53"},{"topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault · CI/CD","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"Your CI pipeline fails during the automated test stage after a code change. How would you investigate the failure, and what does this situation tell you about the role of CI in a development team?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When a CI pipeline fails during automated testing, the first step is to review the pipeline logs to identify which tests failed and understand the error messages. I would check whether the failure is due to a genuine code regression, a flaky test, or an environmental issue. Reproducing the failure locally or in a controlled test environment helps confirm the root cause. From a CI perspective, this situation demonstrates the value of early feedback and that the pipeline is doing its job by preventing defective code from progressing further in the delivery process. Rather than seeing the failure as a blocker, it should be treated as a safety mechanism that protects the shared codebase and reduces downstream risk. Once the issue is fixed, rerunning the pipeline ensures confidence that the change is safe to merge.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-your-ci-pipeline-fails-during-the-automa-54"},{"topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault · CI/CD","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"In a team where many developers commit and merge changes frequently, the main branch becomes unstable. How would you use CI/CD practices to improve stability and collaboration?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>In a high-commit environment, an unstable main branch usually means changes are being integrated without enough automated validation. To address this, I would use CI/CD to introduce strong quality gates before code reaches the main branch. This starts with running automated builds and tests on every pull request so that issues are detected early, before merging.</p>\n<p>I would also enforce branch protection rules that require the CI pipeline to pass and, if applicable, require peer reviews before merging. This prevents broken or incomplete changes from being integrated. Encouraging small, frequent commits helps reduce the risk of conflicts and makes failures easier to identify and fix.</p>\n<p>From a collaboration standpoint, fast and reliable CI feedback is critical. Developers quickly see whether their change is safe, take ownership of fixing failures, and avoid blocking others. Overall, CI/CD helps keep the main branch in a continuously deployable state while improving team confidence and development velocity.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-in-a-team-where-many-developers-commit-a-55"},{"topic":"CI/CD","source":"tech-vault · CI/CD","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/moabukar/tech-vault","q":"A CI/CD pipeline succeeds, but the application fails when deployed to the staging environment. What are the likely causes, and how would you handle it?","answer":[{"type":"html","html":"<p>When a pipeline passes in CI but fails in staging, it usually indicates a difference between environments rather than a problem with the code itself. Common causes include missing or incorrect environment variables, misconfigured secrets, infrastructure differences, or deployment scripts that behave differently outside the CI environment.</p>\n<p>To handle this, I would start by reviewing deployment and application logs in staging, then verify configuration values and infrastructure dependencies. I would also compare staging and CI environments to identify mismatches. This situation highlights why environment parity is important in CI/CD — staging should closely resemble production so issues are caught earlier. Improving configuration management and standardizing environments helps reduce these failures over time.</p>"}],"why":[],"id":"s-a-ci-cd-pipeline-succeeds-but-the-applic-56"}];
